{"lines": ["The Bloop was not alone. Researchers gave a whole family of sounds nicknames, and each has a shape.", "Slow Down. Recorded on the nineteenth of May, 1997, near sixty-two degrees south, off the Antarctic Peninsula. Its pitch falls slowly for about seven minutes, like something rolling to a stop. NOAA says a drifting iceberg slowing as it touches the seafloor makes that signal. It has been recorded many times since, as large icebergs run aground. After a slowdown, a speedup often follows, as the tides move the ice.", "Train. The fourth of March, 1997. A steady tone, around thirty-two to thirty-five hertz. Its direction points to a very large iceberg grounded in the Ross Sea near Cape Adare, dragging its keel across the bottom.", "Julia. The first of March, 1999. Also most likely a large iceberg that ran aground off Antarctica.", "Then Whistle. Seventh of July, 1997. A single undulating tone, much like signals from erupting underwater volcanoes. NOAA could not locate it. Finding a source takes three instruments, and Whistle was heard by only one.", "Rule five. Read the shape. Then admit when you only have one ear."], "words": [["The", 0.0, 0.1], ["Bloop", 0.1, 0.34], ["was", 0.34, 0.54], ["not", 0.54, 0.72], ["alone.", 0.72, 1.04], ["Researchers", 1.9, 1.95], ["gave", 1.95, 2.36], ["a", 2.36, 2.48], ["whole", 2.48, 2.66], ["family", 2.66, 3.02], ["of", 3.02, 3.28], ["sounds", 3.28, 3.48], ["nicknames,", 3.48, 4.02], ["and", 4.02, 4.5], ["each", 4.5, 4.66], ["has", 4.66, 4.88], ["a", 4.88, 5.0], ["shape.", 5.0, 5.26], ["Slow", 6.1, 6.36], ["Down.", 6.36, 6.68], ["Recorded", 6.96, 7.26], ["on", 7.26, 7.56], ["the", 7.56, 7.62], ["nineteenth", 7.62, 8.06], ["of", 8.06, 8.11], ["May,", 8.11, 8.26], ["1997,", 8.26, 9.04], ["near", 9.56, 9.64], ["sixty-two", 9.64, 10.04], ["degrees", 10.04, 10.44], ["south,", 10.44, 10.78], ["off", 10.78, 11.1], ["the", 11.1, 11.24], ["Antarctic", 11.24, 11.58], ["Peninsula.", 11.58, 12.02], ["Its", 12.92, 12.97], ["pitch", 12.97